q-332683/selection/57f3ca22c6160b74c8a487fad49af2b4c3608948b45066955d33067fa39d9af4" "q-332683/selection/0982c2cd670fb5134075230649c22f307927cb42ff6163766746340ccf18ad7d" "q-332683/selection/2670e01ec26c376b718f1627e4de3e662593d56f09d407e828ee3b001482e2f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332683" :question/id #uuid "3f7b6c7b-9bbd-58e1-9f4f-ab6d8faeb7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332683" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332683/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332683"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332579"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/3784f65f97466e17d857a66ca17e69173de72e9608111c839e989cfff3fe10e0.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332713/selection/5d8f5cf460d2c3fe5d1ea7af09a99501f8d26108fa79a59c06a22fcd12a883e1" :answer/id #uuid "7f15fc40-d473-582e-95a0-472217073a74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332713/selection/c8208e0eb9b59858d1566957d052f114a095dae767a69b45f4961b5c02299f2f" :answer/id #uuid "c71d3f5f-7666-5c82-b4ee-95369f2e4c36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332713/selection/c719168ae2cd5ba5e592036d89a6ebb2a7c69729c2d194523601043b746f2400" :answer/id #uuid "c046edaf-62f7-5bd8-b559-666367e693cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332713/selection/90f475b5109a8fc94a8f85535afe7d28a1d2edaeedd5210e9aa2859d1524286f" :answer/id #uuid "282f4dd5-06af-5339-8139-ad22cce8c2f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332713/selection/52299b9115de4b7b6c48be5390afa7587ba810ba68cf2aa497b6da7e08cae55d" :answer/id #uuid "8087793b-ec9f-5620-9575-211579157e4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332713/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0466e64-28eb-5947-af1b-3f5bdfea0d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332713/selection/c719168ae2cd5ba5e592036d89a6ebb2a7c69729c2d194523601043b746f2400" :answer-field/choices ["q-332713/selection/5d8f5cf460d2c3fe5d1ea7af09a99501f8d26108fa79a59c06a22fcd12a883e1" "q-332713/selection/c8