eference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/039bae0d2eedf7d12d8283b2b134855c531efcba1bd0fa14fe9f841b28d5204e.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332717/selection/ma-v1/55d445ed7ca30f236ca7dfa23f28cf1dfbbf69d0a305613a958e3bdc1f1e6699"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722/selection/b9257bcf79c9b6b6f1df28f3fb545fd31be71b6e5207a4b2961107a697a22557" :answer/id #uuid "03c96236-6e1e-51a3-99e9-1aba09ee3ddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722/selection/f427c140b0ca2d63d59c0f9106504d3e7ee5a131093e52099efdc28e02937d7e" :answer/id #uuid "9c371ebc-0600-5990-8052-c1beb76158cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722/selection/654eeaa144e3231741462b34f29c89ff2c725bb49d64a46303ab70dcffdc9940" :answer/id #uuid "4186a912-08f8-5e3a-a5ee-9f1d19e10209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722/selection/0ff90483d173fa9ee8532f287425f67ab73b07066849536f4761749695ae300f" :answer/id #uuid "be342178-890e-51a4-aec7-763396e084c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722/selection/fd87e45eb2e1917fff5983541fb8879158a952836d41d884dc6b2e7236e61ed2" :answer/id #uuid "1bb44816-ac9f-50fb-94bc-5d0edc245bda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "72d93df0-3cf3-5882-90d6-60a03b1d0cba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332722/selection/b9257bcf79c9b6b6f1df28f3fb545fd31be71b6e5207a4b2961107a697a22557" :answer-field/choices ["q-332722/selection/b9257bcf79c9b6b6f1df28f3fb545fd31be71b6e5207a4b2961107a697a22557" "q-332722/selection/f427c140b0ca2d63d59c0f9106504d3e7ee5a131093e52099efdc28e02937d7e" "q-332722/selection/654eeaa144e3231741462b34f29c89ff2c725bb49d64a46303ab70dcffdc9940" "q-332722/selection/0ff90483d173fa9ee8532f287425f67ab73b07066849536f4761749695ae300f" "q-332722/selection/fd87e45eb2e1917fff5983541fb8879158a952836d41d884dc6b2e7236e61ed2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332722" :question/id #uuid "a0f6dfc4-5390-5e5d-ba6a-823b962878f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332722" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a6a2a93d3fdf38eb57b5183a8d8c5d87f8524f976b5c8827e4717c0aeaf69200.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332722/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f