election/5bf4719f118cb9be59a1d34d5a70fdcdef0e903d4819b9e8b82214a79607c0df" "q-332707/selection/ff8034da1c9e899dbde8780c9de4c2519ba25e5f3316bce51e825249bc94c66d" "q-332707/selection/cd603f31b0e323c4df9a17251d74e89c62ea7da83232fb2555cd5529d1e636ba" "q-332707/selection/bdb81ae492b45ab90fee956f1494c216f6ae67ffc1f6eaa98b393b860019c57b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332707" :question/id #uuid "a404c5fa-4d30-52e2-83e1-68532d78d0f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-332707" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/5243b22ebef45709f214ac2422cfb72528f24fd5ef51bd38aa3fc0ff40f1888d.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332707/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332707"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751/selection/f017a4c6ddb5e30cc260057af997ebc8005e5f55daade9391f1f3b841718428c" :answer/id #uuid "523ee55e-3cca-5570-a3c8-88618f38ccf1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/64c0ac27236c4bfae452572e6b5b4c1d6184c45ebac9149ca4fc442d7533bb51.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751/selection/b774953db7ecfadf4caf8fa8a4c6c820124001cd0f9e6d359c1fd71ed1227610" :answer/id #uuid "b2717e8c-9634-59d8-89ca-0556e1b3c415" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9ff104bc583ec025ede6de68f7ea546dea99c914153b8e2325c6f2f9188d0a9b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751/selection/13ce5acf4f19f7dc02557ad777e0caac337c7bc18b4a79ab8af00d186d73c293" :answer/id #uuid "2b9e0825-5c33-5792-aff8-4bed0337d680" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/9fb1f3c545855e7bdfe591574a9069e69bdcc0cc750e5fc4f5aa3eccfdc7ea5a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751/selection/293eccb0d8c87f6dc70018fce7f5be5cc0941fc9bbfba1fece9f9865ff6495e6" :answer/id #uuid "0284ebed-cd7c-560f-ab97-db2904fb4c8f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/eceeabfeb428687bb9f630fe3578937c3fd4fb910605dc25daf56ad5ce18a68a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751/selection/78deb33d28e4d4c7c95eabf43df06d9e1d809cca0479cf3ba7b2f5913e521aec" :answer/id #uuid "bfa90dfd-8d3e-5295-926d-2840eb8b6f81" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "33d486d8-424f-575b-8bd1-60eb83cd9f4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-332751/selection/78deb33d28e4d4c7c95eabf43df06d9e1d809cca0479cf3ba7b2f5913e521aec" :answer-field/choices ["q-332751/selection/f017a4c6ddb5e30cc260057af997ebc8005e5f55daade9391f1f3b841718428c" "q-332751/selection/b774953db7ecfadf4caf8fa8a4c6c820124001cd0f9e6d359c1fd71ed1227610" "q-332751/selection/13ce5acf4f19f7dc02557ad777e0caac337c7bc18b4a79ab8af00d186d73c293" "q-332751/selection/293eccb0d8c87f6dc70018fce7f5be5cc0941fc9bbfba1fece9f9865ff6495e6" "q-332751/selection/78deb33d28e4d4c7c95eabf43df06d9e1d809cca0479cf3ba7b2f5913e521aec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332751" :question/id #uuid "7a71e1e9-c69e-580f-aa68-2f882f086377" :question/math-academy-id "q-332751" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332751/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332751"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184/selection/42327eb017485ddfda20ec50c1d37882af780d34e2ae2d021381b57597947b13" :answer/id #uuid "78b65467-beeb-50a9-9d8e-90f072a5b5ff" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c9bbd9bd8173af0ee9ea41.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184/selection/9da9f87e6e1e5c69abb39cbedb407649753c47e1ef748b23ad69513b456e8415" :answer/id #uuid "8501b0f6-47e4-54bb-bb05-3c822f5a0bde" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/ff9ffa100793101811d8aa6e0e95587ec912ef1e3f64d59fbac617a521479a5d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184/selection/934bfac4c8a5028f161c80e18f3d858d47bacdb74656a0c5246d2b06f80154c3" :answer/id #uuid "818b06b6-bdb5-511f-bf8d-ef51c4130f09" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184/selection/c8d28890b311fa1a73acff44c2206e8aa2845f2b419e4cd39ba7013d753e31fa" :answer/id #uuid "54d88cae-6632-5589-9054-d0e2e12ff77b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/b930f98928e3c4af06434c720d953ad0fcee17500113ed9a72492eda4219a7fd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184/selection/1ac1310600525cdbafbb3b6b7c4ccb7cbd3b06ea9b703a5067c935377bf23cd6" :answer/id #uuid "e18a48f2-a3e6-5993-bd4d-8bc1544e7dbb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/0b2cfbe650613293fe807be4ff7e38fa6f10b2e792dae8f7f4b78f27560f59d7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a10bce-add0-5880-beac-b7920e570aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-333184/selection/934bfac4c8a5028f161c80e18f3d858d47bacdb74656a0c5246d2b06f80154c3" :answer-field/choices ["q-333184/selection/42327eb017485ddfda20ec50c1d37882af780d34e2ae2d021381b57597947b13" "q-333184/selection/9da9f87e6e1e5c69abb39cbedb407649753c47e1ef748b23ad69513b456e8415" "q-333184/selection/934bfac4c8a5028f161c80e18f3d858d47bacdb74656a0c5246d2b06f80154c3" "q-333184/selection/c8d28890b311fa1a73acff44c2206e8aa2845f2b419e4cd39ba7013d753e31fa" "q-333184/selection/1ac1310600525cdbafbb3b6b7c4ccb7cbd3b06ea9b703a5067c935377bf23cd6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333184" :question/id #uuid "91fc904e-97e1-5e34-b331-513c29eb83a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-333184" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333184/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333184"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333177/selection/575d31a8f68e369be20da846abccf96576ad38d3cbb4c2d167dbfdcd42000428" :answer/id #uuid "148d8f21-24e1-51d6-ad23-a847e1a1806a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/a5ae215022638430d0c89d