field/choices ["q-333177/selection/575d31a8f68e369be20da846abccf96576ad38d3cbb4c2d167dbfdcd42000428" "q-333177/selection/0090fd8df42b4b46a655dc982d9b6dc0c0ce558364795813d94e530847fa8157" "q-333177/selection/341350220570f186dad83d901d179e40119d47c6f083d3dc05dd3769735a4985" "q-333177/selection/771e5f9278daf334682a4ccd313b47b739e980d2b0ca34d9d8d1d24512f3969c" "q-333177/selection/b866926e15b7c2e1af3117f73604fd9b4b59d1d2d5addf0322f027f30604e646"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333177" :question/id #uuid "34a4a204-b6f5-559d-8b71-d224e42d358b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333177" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333177/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333177"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333192/selection/e924f1cae3a3926dcf41bd86c1b235df4c89e5924bf63c0d6497df6ba9a4b6a7" :answer/id #uuid "e089de5e-fe0b-53ab-8c53-e682261f6d74" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/71c58730ea546deee365729cab6f1cae3f9aa73409a302067dbbf91fdd85a24d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333192/selection/07cf178743b0dfe20cd2884421be6d32f9166ea03026d23a510852d127850cfe" :answer/id #uuid "ea7c55b8-3874-5d56-a05a-b32c7c1fbfdf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/as