725-f023-588c-a9dd-f148fa0a258b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333218/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "f23a548b-f02b-5a61-b868-beb5ff61d03f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333218/field-3/cef53e2f4f249e823b4999c806f3152f01b5aecabcf33f4f9df9ee55880449dc" :answer-field/choices ["q-333218/field-3/22e8a0aa62bc24395706ed418c76a5ef3df41c0d85ee55b4553fc245a500b4aa" "q-333218/field-3/47b5e16428f6cc903ef4e40f0ded305cf4b2c6071376c9e87a33cc1a98eb9f04" "q-333218/field-3/cef53e2f4f249e823b4999c806f3152f01b5aecabcf33f4f9df9ee55880449dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333218" :question/id #uuid "5736483b-c42f-58a2-a284-3f32820dd4e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333218" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069185/assets/657508496ddc73c386545152b45cc628d9abddd13ec069daf169d1dc61c1f7be.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-333218/field-1" "q-333218/field-2" "q-333218/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-333218"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-333227/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "c944d4cc-c612-5fdc-aeb4-553d1d434e17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333227/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer/id #uuid "ba18efdf-6d49-547e-95bd-8b5c5d795a3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-333227/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "04fdd5b4-1bad-53f7-aae9-7d1d6f62f3f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-333227/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb" :answer-field/choices ["q-333227/field-1/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" "q-333227/field-1/13830b2add0d0975c36840daaa2e8e095b7fd681945e647b89b38b31218d8cdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-333227/field-2/7c90f47a8eeece38813c4ffab6b0911f5ec28b3c4ca7b2ab9be1fd0be059a4f4" :answer/id #uuid "6af05295-1